 > 	Warning: cannot write scoreboard file record

=====

Given that you've gotten booted to the game's exit
by unknown problems, what do the error messages you
SEE mean?

NetHack is written to be a game on a game server,
where many players' games might be ending at the
same time. Letting all of the end processing of
their NetHack games write the the scorefile named
"record" at the same time would turn it into hash,
so NetHack is going to go try to get an exclusive
write lock on that file, by spinning on retries
until it is successful or exceeds the retry count.
See the lock_file(...) routine in src/files.c at
unpatched line 1287 for the ugly details.

Essentially, lock_file creates an empty file, for
unix-like compilations of NetHack given the name
"perm", the detection of which by other instances
of NetHack trying to write the record file keeps
their locking attempts spinning. But you aren't
succeeding at getting a "perm" file written and
owned by "you".

 > 	Hit return to continue:

 > and when I hit return I get:

 > 	No write permission to lock perm!

Trying to create the file named "perm", NetHack runs
into a permissions problem. Somehow, the directory
into which you are trying to write the file named
"perm" isn't a directory into which your NetHack
binary file has permissions to write, perhaps
because you as the user don't have that permission.

Look at the permissions on the NetHack playfield
directory, the one where the files "record" and
"logfile" should live, pretty much the current
directory for the NetHack binary as it runs, and
broaden them as necessary to make sure that you, the
player, rather than just you, the system
administrator "root" or "admin", have write
permissions there, and make sure, as Pasi suggested,
that environmental variable HACKDIR points to that
directory.

